  "caveats": [
    "277,173 cells with uw_class == 'U' (underwriting class *unknown*, not *not applicable*) were held out of this class-conditioned fit. 'NA' rows are retained as their own stratum. Pass keep_unknown_uw_class=True to include them.",
    "61 base strata (0.009% of exposure) had zero deaths across the whole window and were dropped \u2014 their base rate is not estimable and they carry no trend information.",
    "152 cells had zero exposure and were dropped \u2014 a Poisson offset needs exposure * q_base > 0, and a cell nobody was exposed in carries no information. They held 0.0 deaths, as expected.",
    "The A/E section covers the full loaded book (126,223 cells), while the fitted surface covers 125,676 \u2014 the zero-death strata above are excluded from the fit but retained in A/E, because SOA's denominator covers every cell they priced. The two populations differ by 0.009% of exposure.",
    "Duration is controlled for via 9 bands (start policy years [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 11, 16, 21, 26]), each collapsed onto a fixed representative duration so the value is calendar-invariant and cannot reintroduce the mix confound it exists to remove. Selection *within* a band is still pooled \u2014 the first three years are banded singly for that reason.",
    "Pearson dispersion \u03c6 = 1.16, so the quasi-Poisson covariance scaling was applied: every band below is 1.08x wider than a plain-Poisson fit would have reported. The point estimates are unchanged \u2014 scaling touches the covariance only. A \u03c6 this far above 1 means unmodelled heterogeneity, which is expected on real grouped data and is why the scaling is not optional here.",
    "The base offset is estimated from these same cells, so `overall_ae` is ~1 by construction and is not an independent check on the level. The fitted improvement is unaffected: it is a calendar contrast, and every calendar-invariant term \u2014 the offset included \u2014 cancels in the difference."
  ],
